---
import GuidesShell from '../../../components/guides/GuidesShell.astro';

const toc = [
  { id: 'why-svelte', label: 'Why Svelte', level: 2 as const },
  { id: 'why-kotlin', label: 'Why Kotlin', level: 2 as const },
  { id: 'one-catalog-two-runtimes', label: 'One catalog, two runtimes', level: 2 as const },
  { id: 'what-exists-today', label: 'What exists today', level: 2 as const },
];
---

<GuidesShell
  title="Why Svelte and Kotlin"
  description="The two technologies behind Texuli, what each one is responsible for, and how one contract serves both."
  {toc}
>
  <p>
    Texuli renders the web with <strong>Svelte 5</strong> and speaks to native platforms through
    <strong>Kotlin</strong>. This page explains why both were chosen, what each owns, and how the
    same component catalog drives the two runtimes.
  </p>

  <h2 id="why-svelte">Why Svelte</h2>
  <p>
    Texuli ships as compiled source, not as a runtime you download. Svelte is a compiler: a
    component becomes plain JavaScript that touches the DOM directly, with no virtual DOM diffing
    and no framework runtime carried in the bundle. That maps directly onto Texuli's promises:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Performance is structural, not tuned.</strong> There is no reconciliation layer to
      outgrow. Updates land on exactly the nodes that changed, which is what keeps dense surfaces —
      grids, charts, decks — inside their size and interaction budgets.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Small bundles by default.</strong> Each subpath is budgeted in
      <code>texuli.budgets.json</code> and measured by <code>pnpm size</code>; a compiler that
      emits only what the component uses makes those budgets honest ones.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Runes make state explicit.</strong> Svelte 5's <code>$state</code>,
      <code>$derived</code> and <code>$effect</code> put reactivity in the language surface, so
      component contracts stay readable and auditable — important for a library that is designed to
      be read, copied and owned by agents and humans alike.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Fewer ways to shoot yourself.</strong> Snippets over injection holes, no
      <code>innerHTML</code> idiom in the mainstream path, and compile-time warnings on the
      accessibility mistakes Texuli is held to anyway.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="why-kotlin">Why Kotlin</h2>
  <p>
    The web runtime cannot follow Texuli everywhere. Android applications, Compose surfaces, iOS
    sharing and desktop tooling live in a world where a JavaScript bundle is the guest, not the
    host. Kotlin Multiplatform is the bridge:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>One language across the native side.</strong> Shared data models, validation and
      catalog logic compile once per platform instead of being re-written per target.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>First-class tooling.</strong> Static typing, multiplatform libraries and IDE support
      keep the native half held to the same bar as the web half.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>It consumes the contract, not the web code.</strong> Kotlin never imports Svelte
      packages; it reads the generated spec (see below). The layer graph stays one-directional.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="one-catalog-two-runtimes">One catalog, two runtimes</h2>
  <p>
    The seam between the two worlds is the <strong>catalog contract</strong>: every Texuli
    component's props, kinds, enums and tiers are declared once in the manifests, and
    <code>@texuli/spec</code> generates both a TypeScript and a Kotlin projection of that contract
    (<code>packages/spec/generated/typescript/</code> and
    <code>packages/spec/generated/kotlin/com/texuli/spec/</code>). Svelte components implement the
    contract on the web; Kotlin consumes it wherever Svelte cannot go. Agent output rendered on
    either runtime is validated against the same schema with the same URL policy tiers.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-exists-today">What exists today</h2>
  <p>
    Honesty first: the web stack is the implemented one. The Svelte packages, the theme engine,
    the catalog, the spec generator and both generated projections are real and tested. Every
    package is on npm as <code>0.2.0</code>, a pre-1.0 release (<code>latest</code> and
    <code>next</code> both point to it); there is no stable release yet, and 1.0.0 will be the
    first. texuli.com deploys from
    the main branch. The Kotlin Multiplatform side is in development: the Compose renderer
    consumes the Kotlin projection in place from this repository, and nothing is on Maven yet
    (publication under <code>com.texuli</code> is planned in ADR-005).
  </p>
</GuidesShell>
